\section*{Supplementary Table S5: Cross-Tissue Vanilla Baseline Characteristics}
\label{tab:s5_vanilla}

All values from vanilla (prior-free) models under the unified protocol. $\trloss(\Hmat_V)$ is the denominator in the $\kappa$ definition. (The effective-dimensionality context columns from the earlier manuscript draft were removed during the audit because no surviving computation source reproduces them.)

\vspace{0.5cm}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Tissue} & \textbf{Cells} & \textbf{Genes} & \textbf{$\trloss(\Hmat_V)$} \\
\midrule
Hematopoietic & 29,985 & 20,466 & 5,634.8 \\
Neural crest  & 21,078 & 11,932 & 5,402.1 \\
Lung fibrosis & 19,999 & 41,995 & 12,568.7 \\
Human IPF     & 8,532  & 16,299 & 10,590$^{\dagger}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}

\vspace{0.5cm}
$^{\dagger}$Human IPF Hessian trace from the full-transcriptome fp32 vanilla retrain (\texttt{results/ptvae\_human\_ipf\_ln\_v2/optionB\_summary.md}); it is the vanilla denominator for the Section~2.3 retrain and is computed under that protocol, not under the unified StandardScaler/Ridge protocol used for the other three tissues.

\vspace{0.5cm}
\textbf{Key observations:}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $\trloss(\Hmat_V)$ varies $2.3$-fold across tissues ($5{,}402.1$--$12{,}568.7$). This is why tissue-specific normalization is essential: a perturbation of fixed absolute magnitude would produce different $\kappa$ values in different tissues.
\item The lung fibrosis model has the highest $\trloss(\Hmat_V)$ (12,568.7), reflecting the spectral complexity of a high-dimensional fibrosis program. The prior-injected R9 configuration approaches but does not reach the locking threshold ($C=48.2\%$; Table~S1).
\item The neural crest model's $\trloss(\Hmat_V)$ (5,402.1) reflects the intrinsic spectral complexity of neural crest differentiation, which involves multiple concurrent lineage decisions (neural, mesenchymal, melanocytic).
\item Human IPF: full-transcriptome fp32 retraining (BatchNorm and LayerNorm decoders) shows data-inherent deep global compression $C=95.3$--$96.7\%$ with a subcritical gap ratio $\kappa_{\mathrm{gap}}=0.010$--$0.025$ (Methods, Supplementary Note~4): the high $C$ reflects data-inherent concentration, not prior-driven locking.
\end{enumerate}

